package cli

// Text of the generated reference docs (docs/SETTINGS.md and docs/DOCTOR.md). Edit here, then regenerate with
//
//	go test ./internal/cli -run TestReferenceDocs -update
//
// settingAbout is key -> (accepted values, what it does).
var settingAbout = map[string][2]string{
	"OM_LAN_IP": {"`auto`, `off`, or an IPv4 address",
		"The address om treats as this machine's LAN address, where the public tier is served. `auto` detects it; `off` turns the LAN tier off."},
	"OM_VPN": {"`auto`, or interfaces / addresses such as `wg0,10.8.0.1`",
		"The VPNs trusted to reach the private tier: only VPNs whose members are all your own devices. None by default. Set it with `om vpn trust <interface>`."},
	"OM_VPN_ALLOW": {"empty, or addresses and ranges",
		"Only these VPN devices reach the private tier; other devices on a trusted VPN get the public tier. Empty means every device on a trusted VPN. Set it with `om vpn allow <device>`; the host firewall enforces it (`om firewall apply`), and until it does the VPN's private tier stays closed."},
	"OM_NOTIFY": {"`auto`, `off`, or a comma list of `desktop`, `webhook`",
		"Where om sends messages about updates and failures. `auto` is desktop toasts only, so nothing leaves the machine; `webhook` posts to `OM_NOTIFY_URL` and is never automatic. Every message is also written to `notify.log`."},
	"OM_NOTIFY_URL": {"an `https://` URL (`http://` only to this machine or a private address)",
		"Where the webhook channel posts, for example an ntfy topic. It acts like a password: the file is kept 0600 and `om config` shows it as `***`."},
	"OM_TRUSTED_USERS": {"a comma list of account names",
		"Other accounts on this machine that are yours. `om doctor` does not count them as people who could use the private tier without a login."},
	"OM_WALLPAPERS_DIR": {"`auto` or a folder",
		"The folder the dashboard takes wallpapers from. `auto` uses `~/Pictures/Wallpapers` when it exists, else a folder in om's data."},
	"OM_HTTP_PORT": {"1-65535, or `auto`",
		"The host port of the front door (HTTP). `auto`: `om core start` takes 80 if this machine can bind it, else the first free one of 8000, 8888 and 9080, and remembers it, so URLs do not change between starts. Set a number to pin it, for example to run a second om, for another OS user, on the same machine."},
	"OM_HTTPS_PORT": {"1-65535, or `auto`",
		"The host port of the front door (HTTPS). `auto`: 443 if free, else the first free one of 8444, 9443 and 9444, remembered like `OM_HTTP_PORT`."},
	"OM_PRIVATE_PORT_BASE": {"1024-65335, default 20000",
		"The first port of the block of 200 that gives each private web service its own port (and so its own browser origin). Move it when those ports are taken. Together with `OM_PUBLIC_PORT_BASE` and `OM_STREAM_PORT_BASE` the three blocks must not overlap or contain a front-door port; otherwise all three fall back to their defaults and `om doctor` says so. Restart the core after changing it (`om core restart`)."},
	"OM_PUBLIC_PORT_BASE": {"1024-65335, default 21000",
		"The first port of the block of 200 that serves public web services on the LAN and VPN addresses. See `OM_PRIVATE_PORT_BASE`."},
	"OM_STREAM_PORT_BASE": {"1024-65335, default 22000",
		"The first port of the block of 200 that raw TCP stream services (`om.tcp`) get unless they ask for a port. See `OM_PRIVATE_PORT_BASE`."},
	"OM_FIREWALL_EXTRA_TCP": {"a comma list of ports or ranges",
		"Extra inbound TCP ports the host firewall lets through, for example `1714-1764`."},
	"OM_FIREWALL_EXTRA_UDP": {"a comma list of ports or ranges",
		"Extra inbound UDP ports the host firewall lets through."},
	"OM_POLICY": {"`enforce` or `warn`",
		"The deploy policy gate: privileged containers, host namespaces, `ports:`, engine sockets and sensitive mounts. `enforce` refuses the compose file and names the fix; `warn` only reports. See `om policy check`."},
	"OM_SIGNED": {"`auto`, `warn` or `enforce`",
		"Signed deploys: a clean commit must be signed by a key in the workspace's `.om/allowed_signers`. `auto` enforces as soon as git is set up to sign with an SSH key, and warns otherwise. A dirty tree always runs as an unsigned dev generation."},
}

// doctorDocs is what each doctor check verifies, by title: the text of docs/DOCTOR.md.
var doctorDocs = map[string]string{
	"Engine":            "Podman answers, and runs rootless or in a VM (a rootful engine on Linux fails: om's core containers could not write your state folders).",
	"Compose":           "The engine's `compose` command is available (a compose provider for Podman: docker-compose v2 or podman-compose).",
	"Ports":             "A rootless engine on Linux is allowed to bind the front door's ports (80 and 443 by default), and the three port blocks (`OM_PRIVATE_PORT_BASE`, `OM_PUBLIC_PORT_BASE`, `OM_STREAM_PORT_BASE`) are valid: inside 1024-65535, not overlapping, not holding a front-door port.",
	"Core":              "Every core container is running, and no part left over from an older om is still there.",
	"Router":            "The internal router is attached to every workspace and folder network, so it can reach their services.",
	"Edge isolation":    "The edge proxy is on `om_core_net` only: containers on workspace networks cannot reach the private tier.",
	"Published ports":   "No workspace container publishes a host port (`ports:`): everything goes through the edge.",
	"Address drift":     "The addresses the core was started with (LAN, VPN) still match this machine's network.",
	"Private tier":      "The private tier answers on this machine's front door.",
	"DNS rebinding":     "A request with a foreign Host header is refused on the private tier (DNS-rebinding protection).",
	"Router token":      "The router listens on no network (only the edge's socket), and refuses a request without the edge token.",
	"Token not logged":  "The edge token never appears in om's own container logs (an unfiltered error log would carry it).",
	"Engine filter":     "The dashboard must see the engine only through om_sockguard: list allowed, writes refused, no raw socket.",
	"Admin surface":     "Nothing administrative listens where the network can reach it: only the edge ports; Caddy's admin API on loopback inside the container.",
	"Running = lock":    "Every workspace runs what its signed generation and lock say (`om fsck` in each).",
	"Egress":            "Each workspace network: internal (no DNS, no route out) and om_egress enforces source-IP ACLs; cross-service reuse & IP forging blocked.",
	"Project isolation": "A container on one folder's network cannot reach another folder's service (by name or address).",
	"Generations":       "Each workspace: generation log chains, and what runs is a clean, signed commit.",
	"LAN tier":          "The LAN address serves the public tier only: private service ports are closed there and the dashboard is public-scoped.",
	"VPN":               "Which VPNs reach the private tier; tunnels present but not trusted.",
	"VPN isolation":     "A VPN device that sends a *.localhost Host gets nothing: those sites answer this machine only.",
	"Address rules":     "Services restricted by address (om.net.allow / om.net.deny): their rules are in the applied firewall.",
	"Links":             "Cross-workspace links: both sides declared, attached, nothing else on their networks.",
	"Custom routes":     "Services carrying their own Caddy rules (om.caddy): allowed, but always visible.",
	"Healthchecks":      "Running workspace containers without a healthcheck: `om update`, auto-updates and the heal timer can only judge a service by its health.",
	"Stream ports":      "Only declared stream ports (default range 22000-22199 and explicit ports from stream_ports.json) are published on om_stream_edge, and no other container publishes stream ports.",
	"Stream firewall":   "A host firewall rule exists for each declared stream port.",
	"Stream isolation":  "From inside a workspace container, om_stream_router and om_stream_edge are unreachable (expect refused/timeout), and a different workspace's stream target is unreachable.",
	".localhost DNS":    "`*.localhost` names resolve on this OS (browsers always resolve them; some command-line tools need a hosts entry).",
	"Firewall":          "The host firewall (`om firewall`) is active and logs refusals and connections.",
	"Boot":              "Containers restart at boot and the core heals itself (`om boot`).",
	"Login":             "The private tier requires a login, or you are the only person who can log in on this machine.",
	"Private reach":     "Who the private tier answers: this machine, and the VPN's devices (all of them, or the allow-list).",
	"Secret files":      "Files that hold secrets are readable by their owner only (POSIX; Windows follows the profile's ACLs).",
	"State":             "om's program folder must hold no machine state (CA key, edge token, registry).",
	"Registry":          "Every registered workspace's folder still exists.",
	"Orphan routes":     "Routes of workspaces whose folder is gone (deleted without `om remove`).",
}
